virtual agent, so to speak.

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:23.930
And then if there
is an instance where

00:01:23.930 --> 00:01:26.270
it needs to be transferred
to a live agent,

00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:28.010
there's functionality
called Agent Assist

00:01:28.010 --> 00:01:31.220
that uses a real-time keyword
search from the conversation

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:33.233
between the live
agent and the customer

00:01:33.233 --> 00:01:35.150
to pull up suggestions
from the knowledge base

00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:37.280
and help them answer
questions quickly.

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:39.980
As a launch partner,
we help customers

00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:42.690
through the entire
lifecycle of the deployment.

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:44.150
So that's everything
from helping

00:01:44.150 --> 00:01:47.690
them design conversation
flows, programming intents,

00:01:47.690 --> 00:01:50.390
building integrations
with the knowledge base

00:01:50.390 --> 00:01:54.890
CRM systems, anywhere else
that user data might exist.

00:01:54.890 --> 00:01:57.050
And then, also, building
that Agent Assist

00:01:57.050 --> 00:01:57.967
functionality as well.

00:01:57.967 --> 00:01:58.758
LORIN PRICE: Great.

00:01:58.758 --> 00:02:00.300
Thanks for sharing
more about that.

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:03.020
Can you talk more in depth
about the types of technologies

00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:04.052
that Quantiphi uses?

00:02:04.052 --> 00:02:04.760
TAYLOR CYR: Yeah.

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:06.890
So because we've
been using Dialogflow

00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:09.475
since before it was even
actually called Dialogflow,

00:02:09.475 --> 00:02:11.600
we've built a lot of custom
machine learning models

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:15.380
that help you build, manage, and
deploy any of these Dialogflow

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:17.880
or Contact Center AI solutions.

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:19.990
One of them is intent
identification.

00:02:19.990 --> 00:02:22.880
So that takes your
historical call or chat logs,

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:24.500
understands the
ways that users are

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:27.230
asking questions, and
programs that context directly

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:28.520
into the virtual agent.

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:30.840
Another involves the
ingestion of text.

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:35.870
So think a knowledge base, or
a FAQ section on your web page.

00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:39.350
It pulls out questions and
answer pairs there, and again

00:02:39.350 --> 00:02:42.770
makes it directly programmable
into the virtual agent.

00:02:42.770 --> 00:02:46.130
We also work a lot with
the NLP sentiment analysis

00:02:46.130 --> 00:02:48.420
that exists in
Dialogflow, taking it

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:50.660
a step further in what
we call emotion analysis.

00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:53.030
So trying to
understand when users

00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:55.280
are having these
negative feelings,

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:59.060
identifying that earlier, and
helping you intervene before it

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:01.970
starts becoming too
negative of an impact

00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:04.130
on customer satisfaction.

00:03:04.130 --> 00:03:07.280
Lastly, we deal a lot
with integrations.

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:11.120
So having your users or
constituents reach out to you

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:13.400
on whatever platform they
feel comfortable is very

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:15.860
important to creating
value for the people that

00:03:15.860 --> 00:03:17.960
will be interacting
with the solution,

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:22.260
whether that be voice, chat,
messenger platforms, SMS,

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:24.680
Dialogflow has all those
integrations built in

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:25.805
and very easily accessible.

00:03:25.805 --> 00:03:28.388
LORIN PRICE: Can you talk about
some of the specific solutions

00:03:28.388 --> 00:03:30.020
that Quantiphi has
built with CCAI?

00:03:30.020 --> 00:03:30.300
TAYLOR CYR: Yeah.

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:32.500
So when a lot of people
hear Contact Center AI,

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:34.680
they think of the
traditional call center.

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:36.080
While it's a great
tool for that,

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:39.590
we found that the NLP
capabilities behind Dialogflow

00:03:39.590 --> 00:03:43.343
are actually useful in a lot
of other situations as well.

00:03:43.343 --> 00:03:45.260
There was some work that
we did in conjunction

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:48.830
with Google Cloud and
Penn State World Campus

00:03:48.830 --> 00:03:53.300
where we used the NLP
capabilities of Dialogflow.

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:54.950
It identified four
different types

00:03:54.950 --> 00:03:57.860
of student requests, where
advisors were spending up

00:03:57.860 --> 00:04:00.800
to 5,000 hours per year
really just pulling

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:03.080
different pieces of
information, determining

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:05.510
a student's eligibility
to change major, campus,

00:04:05.510 --> 00:04:06.510
something like that.

00:04:06.510 --> 00:04:08.630
So we use Dialogflow
to recognize

00:04:08.630 --> 00:04:12.200
the intents of those users,
provide a template in response

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:14.540
to the advisor, that can
then be personalized based

00:04:14.540 --> 00:04:17.000
on the student, all while
pulling all of that data

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:18.529
that they had to
spend 15 minutes

00:04:18.529 --> 00:04:20.870
gathering, putting it
right in front of them,

00:04:20.870 --> 00:04:24.050
and giving them the opportunity
to add value with their time,

00:04:24.050 --> 00:04:25.727
rather than just
searching for data.

00:04:25.727 --> 00:04:27.560
LORIN PRICE: What are
some of the challenges

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:29.900
that you've encountered when
building these solutions?

00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:30.170
TAYLOR CYR: Yeah.

00:04:30.170 --> 00:04:31.970
So the first one
that comes to mind

00:04:31.970 --> 00:04:34.010
is designing conversation flows.

00:04:34.010 --> 00:04:36.320
Not always do
customers understand

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:38.630
what they want to
automate and how they want

00:04:38.630 --> 00:04:39.980
the agent to say it.

00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:42.980
So we found that, with
using the topic modeler,

00:04:42.980 --> 00:04:45.350
you cannot only find a lot
of the high-frequency topics,

00:04:45.350 --> 00:04:48.800
but you can dig down a level,
understand within those topics

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:51.650
what people are talking
about, and help structure

00:04:51.650 --> 00:04:54.170
the conversation flow of the
virtual agent using that.

00:04:54.170 --> 00:04:56.540
We've also seen
intent clashing a lot.

00:04:56.540 --> 00:04:58.907
So as you continue to
add intents to the bot,

00:04:58.907 --> 00:05:00.740
it becomes more and
more likely that there's

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:03.630
going to be similarities
between an existing intent.

00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:05.630
So we've created an
automated solution testing

00:05:05.630 --> 00:05:09.757
tool that goes through and tests
those intents every so often,

00:05:09.757 --> 00:05:11.590
and gives you an early
indication if there's

00:05:11.590 --> 00:05:14.810
going to be problems
with a previous intent

00:05:14.810 --> 00:05:17.630
so that you can intervene
and fix that before it ever

00:05:17.630 --> 00:05:18.840
sees the light of day.

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:20.280
LORIN PRICE: Well, this
has been very interesting.

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:22.248
Thank you so much for
being here with us today.

00:05:22.248 --> 00:05:23.540
TAYLOR CYR: It was my pleasure.

